<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bilbilis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augusta Bilbilis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bilbilis was a Celtiberian and then Roman town on a hill near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> in Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It became a Roman municipium under Augustus, with a forum, temple and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and was known for the iron worked from local ores. The poet Martial was born there around 40 CE and praised his home town in his epigrams.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bilbilis.jpg" alt="Bilbilis" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_romana_de_Bilbilis,_Calatayud,_Espa%C3%B1a_2012-05-16,_DD_05.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Celtiberian settlement stood on the heights of Cerro de Bambola and part of San Paterno, north of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeda">Segeda</a> and 60 kilometres south-west of the Roman colony of Caesaraugusta, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. It was the capital of the Lusones, a Celtic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>. Its earliest coins, of the late 2nd to the early 1st century BCE, show a male head with a dolphin and a horseman carrying a spear. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> first met the Lusones in the 2nd century BCE, when Quintus Fulvius Flaccus journeyed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celtiberia">Celtiberia</a>.</p><p>Under Augustus the town became the municipium of Augusta Bilbilis, whose inhabitants all received Roman citizenship, and the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a> to Caesaraugusta passed nearby. Its mint struck coins under Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>, among them one naming Lucius Aelius Sejanus as consul; after his fall his name was filed or stamped off most of them. The city's heyday was the 1st century CE. It declined rapidly in the 2nd century and was half-deserted by the 3rd.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The hilly ground imposed a terraced layout of steep streets and ramps rather than the usual Roman grid. The forum, practically square, lay before the temple in the upper city, framed by porticos, a basilica, a curia and a crypto-porticus; it was completed under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and altered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, linked to the forum by gates and corridors, held around 4,500 spectators, far more than the town's estimated 3,500 inhabitants, so it probably served the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Baths</a> and a nymphaeum fed by hydraulic tanks gave the city a permanent water supply.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The modern town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> was founded near the Roman site. Much of the Museum of Calatayud holds finds from Bilbilis: frescoes from the baths and town houses, coins from its mint, and portraits of the Julio-Claudian imperial family found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> area, among them a veiled head of Augustus found in 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bilbilis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Iron was mined in the Moncayo range and around <b>Bilbilis</b> and Turiaso, and Celtiberian smiths were well known: Diodorus says they buried iron plates until the weaker parts rusted away and forged weapons from what remained.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Latin culture flourished: the rhetorician Quintilian came from Calagurris (Calahorra) and the poet Martial from <b>Bilbilis</b>, near Calatayud.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
